From b7f9ed20c329bc51bbda4f9186da3f7d2a1671a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Frdlik <michaelf.ms@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 09:03:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 07/23] Thylacine Warp-4: the direct-present client half
 (mutual adoption)

The compositor (tapestryd) can now display a GL client's color resource
itself -- fullscreen as a SET_SCANOUT of the 3D resource, windowed as a
server-side readback + compose -- once BOTH halves of a mutual adoption
handshake land: the tapestry surface names the warp ctx ("glsrc <ctx>")
and the warp ctx consents back naming surface + BO ("present-to
<surface> <bo>"). This commit is the client half:

- warp_client: warp_present_to / warp_present_off write the consent verb
  on the ctx ctl (pure naming; geometry and liveness gate ACTIVITY
  per-present server-side, so a resize racing the handshake degrades to
  inactive instead of failing the verb).
- winsys: virgl_thylacine_present_to(vws, hw_res, surface, *ctx_pub_out)
  -- mutex-guarded like every vtable entry; res NULL = withdraw.
- osmesa target: osmesa_thyla_present_bridge digs the driver-private
  hw_res out of a pipe_resource, gated FIRST on the driver name (casting
  a llvmpipe screen to virgl types is UB; a debug-wrapped screen fails
  the gate and the caller keeps the readback path).
- st/osmesa: OSMesaThylacineDirect(ctx, surface_id, *ctx_pub_out)
  resolves the CURRENT front color buffer, places the consent, and
  suppresses the flush_front snapshot while active (the server owns
  display; the user buffer is dead weight). OSMesaThylacineDirectOff
  withdraws + restores the readback. A single per-process owner: the
  warp ctx is per-process (the shared screen), so a second GL context
  negotiating steals the consent and the loser's readback resumes --
  else its pane would freeze on the last suppressed frame. The bridge
  rides a weak reference so software-only targets keep building; the
  osmesa target's object is always extracted in real links (osmesa.o's
  strong osmesa_create_screen reference), verified by nm at fetch.

Re-negotiation is per-bind (the SDL side re-calls on every MakeCurrent /
reweave rebind): the consent names one BO, and a resize reallocates the
framebuffer into a new one.

Alternative considered: a minted-token capability (Fuchsia
BufferCollection-style). Rejected -- mutual naming through two already
owner-gated ctls needs no RNG, no secret storage, and revokes naturally
on either side's death (the server pins the surface incarnation by gen,
so slot reuse cannot re-arm a stale consent).
